{"reference":"Genesis 20","book":"Genesis","chapter":20,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"chapter_overview","title":"Overview","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Abraham’s presentation of Sarah as his sister is repeated in Gerar, but this episode has its own king, setting, dream warning, and restoration. Abimelech insists that he acted without knowing Sarah was married, and the dream both confirms his claim of integrity and identifies the danger created by the deception.","verse_refs":["Genesis 20:1-3","Genesis 20:3-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_genesis_020_6bda02e71243f31e620d326a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_2","text":"The resolution is public as well as personal: Abimelech returns Sarah, gives Abraham livestock, servants, and silver, and offers him a place to live. These gifts function as a material settlement and public resolution, rather than supplying an exact legal schedule for damages. Abraham is also called a prophet here because he is to pray for Abimelech so that he may live; the title highlights his intercession without requiring later institutional ideas about prophecy.","verse_refs":["Genesis 20:7-17","Genesis 20:14-16"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_genesis_020_6bda02e71243f31e620d326a"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]},{"kind":"cultural_context","title":"Household Healing","blocks":[{"id":"block_3","text":"The chapter attributes the closing of the women’s wombs in Abimelech’s household to YHWH because of Sarah, and then reports healing after Abraham’s prayer. It gives no medical mechanism; the account presents the household’s condition and restoration as part of the episode’s divine judgment and resolution.","verse_refs":["Genesis 20:17-18"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_genesis_020_9308ec73cece28ea70c039d2"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading Genesis in Its Larger Setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"Genesis moves from the universal origins narratives of chapters 1–11 into the ancestral family narratives of chapters 12–50, ending with Jacob’s household in Egypt. Genesis 20 belongs within that extended family narrative rather than standing as an isolated account.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_genesis_020_5211ade5c9c8af05bed6476f"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"fact"},{"id":"block_5","text":"Genesis itself does not narrate how its literary form came to be. Mosaic attribution and compositional models are distinct ways readers have approached that question, while the lack of a direct archaeological identification cannot by itself prove or disprove every ancestral narrative; archaeology and literary interpretation address different questions.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_genesis_020_bedfb87bbd12407296d293ef"],"confidence":"low","interpretation_level":"disputed"},{"id":"block_6","text":"In a later argument, Paul explicitly describes his construction involving Hagar and Sarah as an allegory, linking women, sons, mountains, covenants, and Jerusalems. The details of that argument remain contested, so it should be recognized as a later interpretive use rather than simply treated as the meaning of Genesis 20 itself.","provenance_refs":["reader_path_genesis_020_c77c866ccb925be9f545f510"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed"}]}],"generated_metadata":null}